✂️ 2. Text Splitters

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 What is a Text Splitter?

```text id="splitter_def"
A tool that divides large text into smaller chunks
```

---

🎯 Why Text Splitters are Needed

✔ LLM context windows are limited
✔ Smaller chunks improve retrieval
✔ Easier embedding generation

---

🔹 Popular Text Splitters in LangChain

* CharacterTextSplitter
* RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter
* TokenTextSplitter
* MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter

---

📘 CharacterTextSplitter

Splits based on character count.

---

💻 Example

```python id="character_splitter"
from langchain.text_splitter import CharacterTextSplitter

splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(
    chunk_size=500,
    chunk_overlap=50
)

chunks = splitter.split_text(text)
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📘 RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter

Most commonly used splitter.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 How It Works

Tries separators in order:

1. Paragraphs
2. Lines
3. Spaces
4. Characters

---

💻 Example

```python id="recursive_splitter"
from langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter

splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(
    chunk_size=1000,
    chunk_overlap=200
)
```

---

📘 TokenTextSplitter

Splits based on token count instead of characters.

Useful because LLMs process tokens.

---

📘 MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter

Specialized for Markdown documents.

Preserves headings and sections.

---

⚔️ Comparison of Splitters

| Splitter                       | Best For             |
| ------------------------------ | -------------------- |
| CharacterTextSplitter          | Simple text          |
| RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter | General RAG systems  |
| TokenTextSplitter              | Token-aware chunking |
| MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter     | Markdown docs        |

---

🔄 Workflow in RAG

```text id="rag_splitter_flow"
Documents → Text Splitter → Chunks → Embeddings → Vector Store
```

---

🔧 Best Practices

✔ Use overlap to preserve context
✔ Keep chunks semantically meaningful
✔ Store metadata with every chunk
✔ Tune chunk size experimentally

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Very large chunks
❌ No overlap
❌ Ignoring metadata
❌ Inconsistent chunk sizes

---

🌍 Real-World Applications

✔ Enterprise search systems
✔ PDF chatbots
✔ Legal document assistants
✔ Research tools

---

🧠 Interview Answer (Simple)

> Metadata filtering improves retrieval relevance by filtering documents based on attributes like source or category, while text splitters divide large documents into smaller chunks for embedding and retrieval.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ Metadata provides additional document information
✔ Metadata filtering improves retrieval precision
✔ Text splitters create manageable chunks
✔ RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter is most commonly used
✔ Both are essential for high-quality RAG systems

